<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulayman al-Baruni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Suleiman al-Baruni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulayman al-Baruni was an Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> scholar and politician from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Nafusa Mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. A member of the Ottoman parliament, he led resistance to the Italian invasion from 1911 and in 1918 was one of the leaders of the Tripolitanian Republic. He later lived in exile and served as an adviser in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulayman-al-baruni.jpg" alt="Sulayman al-Baruni" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A7%D9%84%D9%85%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%87%D8%AF_%D8%B3%D9%84%D9%8A%D9%85%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B1%D9%88%D9%86%D9%8A.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Baruni was born around 1870 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Jabal Nafusa</a>, then part of the Ottoman vilayet of Tripolitania, into an influential <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> family of the Ibadi sect. His father, Abd Allah al-Baruni, was a jurist, poet and theologian. From 1887 he studied at the University of Ez-Zitouna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, where the reformist Muhammad al-Nakhli, a critic of colonialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>, influenced him strongly. In 1893 he entered Al-Azhar University for three years, met anti-colonial currents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and admired the ideas of Mustafa Kamil, and then studied Ibadi theology and literature in the M'zab valley under Muhammad ibn Yusuf Atfayyash.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> he founded a newspaper and later a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press. Under Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> the Ottoman authorities arrested him several times on the charge of planning to restore an Ibadi imamate or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Jabal Nafusa</a>. After the Young Turk Revolution he was elected to the Chamber of Deputies in 1908 as the member for the Jabal Gharbi. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> broke out in 1911 he began recruiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> against the invasion, and in late October 1912, after the Ottoman capitulation, he took a leading part at the Congress of Aziziyya.</p><p>He then sought an understanding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, hoping for an autonomous Ibadi principality centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafusa-mountains">Jabal Nafusa</a> and Marsa Zuaga, or at least special privileges for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>. After the last Berber resistance was crushed at the Battle of Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaba">Asaba</a>'a on 23 March 1913 he went into exile in French Tunisia. In October 1916, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the Ottoman sultan appointed him governor of Tripolitania, Tunisia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, territories the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> did not actually control. After joining the leadership of the Tripolitanian Republic in November 1918, he made his peace with Italy when the Fundamental Law was issued in June 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By September 1921 Italian policy had helped bring about a civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and many Berbers blamed al-Baruni. He left in November 1921 for his final exile, travelling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> before settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. He died on 1 May 1940 while visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Oman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-bin-taimur">Said bin Taimur</a>. His daughter, Za'ima bint Sulayman, published some of his papers at Tripoli in 1964, and in 1970 his body was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and buried in Tripoli after a national ceremony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulayman al-Baruni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its leadership was a council including <b>Sulayman al-Baruni</b>, Ramadan al-Suwayhili of Misrata, Ahmad al-Murayyid and Abd al-Nabi Bilkhayr.</blockquote><a href="/state/tripolitanian-republic">Tripolitanian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jadu, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.95,12.02&amp;year=1915" aria-label="Jadu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4859 1001 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sulayman al-Baruni"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5309 1271h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5309 1271h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.95,12.02&amp;year=1915">What was here in 1915? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
